.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt PROVIDER-STOREMGMT 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm provider-storemgmt
.Nd The OSSL_STORE library \(la-\(ra provider functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/core_dispatch.h
.Ft /*
  * None of these are actual functions, but are displayed like this for
  * the function signatures for functions that are offered as function
  * pointers in OSSL_DISPATCH arrays.
  */

 void *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_open
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fa "const char *uri"
.Fc
.Ft void *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_attach
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fa "OSSL_CORE_BIO *bio"
.Fc
.Ft const OSSL_PARAM *
.Fo store_settable_ctx_params
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_set_ctx_params
.Fa "void *loaderctx"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_load
.Fa "void *loaderctx"
.Fa "OSSL_CALLBACK *object_cb"
.Fa "void *object_cbarg"
.Fa "OSSL_PASSPHRASE_CALLBACK *pw_cb"
.Fa "void *pw_cbarg"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_eof
.Fa "void *loaderctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_close
.Fa "void *loaderctx"
.Fc
.Ft int OSSL_FUNC_store_export_object
    
.Fo 
.Fa "void *loaderctx"
.Fa "const void *objref"
.Fa "size_t objref_sz"
.Fa "OSSL_CALLBACK *export_cb"
.Fa "void *export_cbarg"
.Fc
.Ft void *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_open_ex
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fa "const char *uri"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fa "OSSL_PASSPHRASE_CALLBACK *pw_cb"
.Fa "void *pw_cbarg"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_store_delete
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fa "const char *uri"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fa "OSSL_PASSPHRASE_CALLBACK *pw_cb"
.Fa "void *pw_cbarg"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The STORE operation is the provider side of the
.Xr ossl_store 7
API.
.Pp
The primary responsibility of the STORE operation is to load all sorts
of objects from a container indicated by URI. These objects are given to
the OpenSSL library in provider-native object abstraction form (see
.Xr provider-object 7 Ns ).
The OpenSSL library is then responsible for passing on that abstraction
to suitable provided functions.
.Pp
Examples of functions that the OpenSSL library can pass the abstraction
to include
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_load 3
.Pf ( Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ),
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_store_export_object 3
(which exports the object in parameterized form).
.Pp
All "functions" mentioned here are passed as function pointers between
.Pa libcrypto
and the provider in
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays via
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
arrays that are returned by the provider's
.Xr provider_query_operation 3
function (see
.Xr provider-base 7 Ns ).
.Pp
All these "functions" have a corresponding function type definition
named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name}_fn ,
and a helper function to retrieve the function pointer from a
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
element named
.Sy OSSL_get_{name} .
For example, the "function"
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_attach
has these:
.Bd -literal
 typedef void *(OSSL_FUNC_store_attach_fn)(void *provctx,
                                           OSSL_CORE_BIO * bio);
 static ossl_inline OSSL_FUNC_store_attach_fn
     OSSL_FUNC_store_attach(const OSSL_DISPATCH *opf);
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays are indexed by numbers that are provided as macros in
.Xr openssl-core_dispatch.h 7 ,
as follows:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_FUNC_store_open                 OSSL_FUNC_STORE_OPEN
 OSSL_FUNC_store_attach               OSSL_FUNC_STORE_ATTACH
 OSSL_FUNC_store_settable_ctx_params  OSSL_FUNC_STORE_SETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_store_set_ctx_params       OSSL_FUNC_STORE_SET_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_store_load                 OSSL_FUNC_STORE_LOAD
 OSSL_FUNC_store_eof                  OSSL_FUNC_STORE_EOF
 OSSL_FUNC_store_close                OSSL_FUNC_STORE_CLOSE
 OSSL_FUNC_store_export_object        OSSL_FUNC_STORE_EXPORT_OBJECT
 OSSL_FUNC_store_delete               OSSL_FUNC_STORE_DELETE
 OSSL_FUNC_store_open_ex              OSSL_FUNC_STORE_OPEN_EX
.Ed
.Ss Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_open
should create a provider side context with data based on the input
.Fa uri .
The implementation is entirely responsible for the interpretation of the
URI.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_attach
should create a provider side context with the core
.Sy BIO
.Fa bio
attached.
This is an alternative to using a URI to find storage, supporting
.Xr OSSL_STORE_attach 3 .
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_store_settable_ctx_params 3
should return a constant array of descriptor
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3 ,
for parameters that
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_set_ctx_params
can handle.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_set_ctx_params
should set additional parameters, such as what kind of data to expect,
search criteria, and so on.
More on those below, in
.Sx Load Parameters .
Whether unrecognised parameters are an error or simply ignored is at the
implementation's discretion.
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_load
loads the next object from the URI opened by
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_open ,
creates an object abstraction for it (see
.Xr provider-object 7 Ns ),
and calls
.Fa object_cb
with it as well as
.Fa object_cbarg .
.Fa object_cb
will then interpret the object abstraction and do what it can to wrap it
or decode it into an OpenSSL structure.
In case a passphrase needs to be prompted to unlock an object,
.Fa pw_cb
should be called.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_eof
indicates if the end of the set of objects from the URI has been
reached.
When that happens, there's no point trying to do any further loading.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_close
frees the provider side context
.Em ctx .
.Pp
When a provider-native object is created by a store manager it would be
unsuitable for direct use with a foreign provider.
The export function allows for exporting the object to that foreign
provider if the foreign provider supports the type of the object and
provides an import function.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_store_export_object 3
should export the object of size
.Fa objref_sz
referenced by
.Fa objref
as an
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array and pass that to the
.Fa export_cb
as well as the given
.Fa export_cbarg .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_delete
deletes the object identified by the
.Fa uri .
The implementation is entirely responsible for the interpretation of the
URI. In case a passphrase needs to be prompted to remove an object,
.Fa pw_cb
should be called.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_open_ex
is an extended variant of
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_open .
If the provider does not implement this function the code internally
falls back to use the original
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_open .
This variant additionally accepts an
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
object and a
.Fa pw_cb
callback that can be used to request a passphrase in cases where the
whole store needs to be unlocked before performing any load operation.
.Ss Load Parameters
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqexpect" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_EXPECT Ns ) <integer>
Is a hint of what type of data the OpenSSL library expects to get.
This is only useful for optimization, as the library will check that the
object types match the expectation too.
.Pp
The number that can be given through this parameter is found in
.Pa <openssl/store.h> ,
with the macros having names starting with
.Qo Li OSSL_STORE_INFO_ Qc .
These are further described in
.Xr OSSL_STORE_INFO 3 .
.It \(dqsubject" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_SUBJECT Ns ) <octet string>
Indicates that the caller wants to search for an object with the given
subject associated.
This can be used to select specific certificates by subject.
.Pp
The contents of the octet string is expected to be in DER form.
.It \(dqissuer" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_ISSUER Ns ) <octet string>
Indicates that the caller wants to search for an object with the given
issuer associated.
This can be used to select specific certificates by issuer.
.Pp
The contents of the octet string is expected to be in DER form.
.It \(dqserial" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_SERIAL Ns ) <integer>
Indicates that the caller wants to search for an object with the given
serial number associated.
.It \(dqdigest" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_DIGEST Ns ) <UTF8 string>
.It \(dqfingerprint" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_FINGERPRINT Ns ) <octet string>
Indicates that the caller wants to search for an object with the given
fingerprint, computed with the given digest.
.It \(dqalias" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_ALIAS Ns ) <UTF8 string>
Indicates that the caller wants to search for an object with the given
alias (some call it a "friendly name").
.It \(dqproperties" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_PROPERTIES Ns ) <utf8 string>
Property string to use when querying for algorithms such as the
.Sy OSSL_DECODER
decoder implementations.
.It \(dqinput-type" ( Ns Sy OSSL_STORE_PARAM_INPUT_TYPE Ns ) <utf8 string>
Type of the input format as a hint to use when decoding the objects in
the store.
.El
.Pp
Several of these search criteria may be combined.
For example, to search for a certificate by issuer+serial, both the
"issuer" and the "serial" parameters will be given.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr provider 7
.Sh HISTORY
The STORE interface was introduced in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_store_delete
callback was added in OpenSSL 3.2
